      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition starts the verification test process — for example, is it triggered by a scheduled time, a manual request from an engineer, or an automated signal from an upstream process such as a code commit or build completion?",
      "answer": "The process begins when a formal test request arrives. The verification engineer first reads through that incoming request along with any associated conditions before doing anything else. The exact mechanism that generates the request — whether scheduled, manual, or automated — is not specified in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What specific activities does the engineer perform during test preparation — for instance, selecting test cases, configuring environment parameters, or loading input data — and are any of these steps already documented in a checklist or template?",
      "answer": "After reviewing the incoming request, the engineer sets up the evaluation environment. An evaluation setup guide exists as a reference document to support this step. The finer details of what configuring the environment entails — such as parameter selection or data loading — are not explicitly described beyond what that guide covers."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What criteria or thresholds does the engineer use to decide that a result 'looks wrong' — are there defined pass/fail rules, numerical tolerances, or is the judgment entirely subjective?",
      "answer": "The engineer examines the collected output files for two things: first, whether the files are fully present and can be read without errors; second, whether the data inside shows unusual or abnormal patterns. These are the two decision points used. Specific numerical thresholds or formal pass/fail rules are not detailed in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "When an investigation is triggered, what does the engineer actually do — and does the investigation ever result in re-running the test, or does it always end with a conclusion being documented?",
      "answer": "An unclear abnormal pattern is listed as a recognized exception, but the workflow does not describe a dedicated investigation activity. Abnormal findings feed into the summary report rather than a separate investigation step. Whether the test gets re-executed as part of resolving issues is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Who are the 'related people' that receive the investigation result, how are they identified for a given test, and through what channel is the information shared with them?",
      "answer": "Once the engineer finishes the written summary, it is passed along to a review engineer, who reads it and may ask for clarification. A project owner is also listed as a participant. Sharing happens through an email or collaboration platform. How the specific recipients are identified for a given test is not described."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What does the short report contain, what format is it produced in, and where does it go after it is written — for example, is it stored in a shared repository, emailed to a specific group, or attached to a ticket?",
      "answer": "The engineer compiles findings into a structured summary using a predefined report template. The output includes a summary document and an accompanying list of identified issues. After drafting, the report is sent to the review engineer via an email or collaboration tool. Storage in a central repository is mentioned as a system but the exact routing after review is not specified."
    }
  ]
}
